        <div class="pb-[10px]">
            <h3 class="text-display-24 md:text-display-32 font-semibold">Conclusion: From Food Security to Agrifood Systems Transformation</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-[10px] font-light">
            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">The State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World 2026 (SOFI 2026) sends a powerful message to policymakers: ending hunger is no longer sufficient. The challenge for the coming decade is to transform agrifood systems so that they deliver healthy, affordable, safe, and sustainable diets while remaining resilient to climate change, economic shocks, and emerging global crises (FAO et al. 2026).</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">Although global hunger has declined for three consecutive years, progress remains too slow to achieve Sustainable Development Goal 2 (Zero Hunger) by 2030. Approximately 645 million people remain undernourished, 2.1 billion people continue to experience moderate or severe food insecurity, and 2.69 billion people cannot afford a healthy diet (FAO et al. 2026). These statistics demonstrate that food security can no longer be measured solely by the quantity of food produced. Rather, it must be evaluated through the lens of nutrition, affordability, resilience, sustainability, and equity.</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">For Southeast Asia, the urgency is even greater. The region faces converging pressures from climate change, rapid urbanization, changing dietary patterns, natural resource degradation, biodiversity loss, and widening socioeconomic inequalities. While ASEAN has achieved significant progress in agricultural production and poverty reduction, millions of people continue to experience food insecurity and malnutrition in its multiple forms. These realities underscore the need to move beyond production-oriented agricultural policies toward integrated agrifood systems approaches that simultaneously improve productivity, nutrition, environmental sustainability, and rural livelihoods (ASEAN 2025; FAO 2025).</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">The encouraging news is that Southeast Asia is well positioned to accelerate this transformation. The adoption of the ASEAN Food, Agriculture and Forestry Sectoral Plan (2026–2030) provides a coherent regional framework that closely aligns with the recommendations of SOFI 2026. Both emphasize climate resilience, sustainable agriculture, nutrition security, digital transformation, regional cooperation, and innovation as key pillars of future food systems (ASEAN 2025).</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">Achieving these ambitions, however, will require stronger investments in agricultural research and development, science-informed policymaking, digital agriculture, climate-smart technologies, resilient food value chains, and human capital development. It will also require closer collaboration among governments, research institutions, universities, development organizations, farmers, civil society, and the private sector.</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">In this context, SEARCA has a strategic opportunity to serve as a regional catalyst for agrifood systems transformation. By strengthening the science–policy–practice interface, generating evidence for policymaking, advancing digital agriculture, promoting climate-smart innovations, developing future agricultural leaders, and fostering regional collaboration, SEARCA can help ASEAN Member States translate regional commitments into measurable improvements in food security, nutrition, resilience, and inclusive rural development.</p>

            <p class="text-display-14 md:text-display-16">Ultimately, achieving food security is no longer simply about producing more food it is about building agrifood systems that nourish people, sustain the planet, and create resilient rural economies. As SOFI 2026 reminds us, transforming food systems is not only essential for ending hunger but also for achieving healthier populations, more prosperous farming communities, and a sustainable future for Southeast Asia and beyond (FAO et al. 2026).</p>
